// mimecode.nv — the well-known media types as integers.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// THIS MODULE IS THE DEVICE HALF, and the integer is the reason it
// exists.  A firmware HTTP server answering `GET /logo.png` off a flash
// filesystem needs one thing from this package — the six bytes
// `image/png` — and what it cannot do is build them: a `MimeType` holds
// spans into a string, and a device that has no heap has no string to
// span.  So the surface a device uses answers a CODE, every comparison
// is an integer compare, and `code_name` is the one call that produces
// text — from a constant, so it is in flash rather than in RAM.
//
// IT IS ALSO THE HOST'S FAST PATH, and that is not a coincidence.  A
// static-file server comparing `Content-Type` strings per request is
// comparing the same twenty strings over and over; comparing codes is
// an integer test, and the code is what `mimeext` answers and what the
// sniffer settles on.  The two halves are one vocabulary rather than
// two.
//
// THE SET IS THE ONE THE SNIFFING STANDARD AND THE EXTENSION TABLE
// SHARE.  A code exists for a type if something in this package can
// produce it without being told: the WHATWG pattern table's outputs,
// and the extensions a web server actually serves.  Everything else is
// a `MimeType` parsed from a string, and `unknown()` is the code that
// says so — which is a real answer rather than a failure, because
// `application/octet-stream` is what a server sends when it does not
// know.

// The code for a type nothing here recognises.
//
// Its name is `application/octet-stream`, which is the honest answer
// rather than an error: a server that does not know what a file is says
// so with that type, and a browser that receives it offers a download
// instead of guessing.
pub fn unknown() -> Int []
    todo("mime-nv.mimecode.unknown")

// The codes, one function each.
//
// Functions rather than a `const` block or an enum, and the reason is
// the device: an enum is not a position a `@value` sniffer's state can
// occupy (SPEC § 14.5), and these have to be readable from inside one.
// A function returning a literal compiles to the literal.

// `text/html`.
pub fn text_html() -> Int []
    todo("mime-nv.mimecode.text_html")

